      <!-- Item Summary -->
      <div id="summary" class="section metadataSection">

        

        <div class="section__header sh">
          <h2 class="sh__main">Summary</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="section__content">
          

          
            <p class="small">
              <strong>Short Citation</strong><br>
              <span>Office of Coast Survey, 2026: Maritime Limits and Boundaries of United States of America, https://www.fisheries.noaa.gov/inport/item/39963.</span><br>
              <a href="/inport/item/39963/citation">Full Citation Examples</a>
            </p>
          

          

          
            
              <strong>Abstract</strong><br>
            
            
            <div class="small">
              
  <p>These limits and boundaries are measured from the official U.S. baseline, which is recognized as the low-water line along the coast as marked on NOAA nautical charts, in accordance with the articles of the Law of the Sea.</p>
  <p>NOAA is responsible for delineating the 12 nautical mile Territorial Sea, 24 nautical mile Contiguous Zone, and 200 nautical mile Exclusive Economic Zone (EEZ) on its nautical charts. The outer limit of each zone is measured from the U.S. normal baseline, which is the low water line on NOAA charts and includes closing lines across legal bays and rivers, consistent with international law. The U.S. Baseline Committee, an interagency body chaired by the U.S. Department of State, reviews and approves the U.S. baseline and associated maritime limits. The Committee&#39;s purpose is to gain interagency consensus on the baseline&#39;s accurate placement, in adherence with the 1958 Convention on the Territorial Sea and the Contiguous Zone and ensure that the seaward extent of U.S. maritime zones remains within the breadth permitted by international law.</p>
  <p>In 2002, and in response to increasing requests for digital maritime zones, NOAA, in partnership with other federal agencies via the U.S. Baseline Committee, launched a project to re-evaluate the U.S. baseline. The baseline evaluation centered on NOAA&#39;s most detailed and current nautical charts, along with supplemental source materials used to verify specific charted features. This dataset is a direct result of this ongoing 2002 initiative. It reflects a multi-year, iterative project during which the baseline and associated maritime limits were re-evaluated on a state or regional basis. In addition to U.S. maritime limits, the dataset also includes U.S. maritime boundaries with opposite or adjacent countries and the U.S./Canada International Boundary (both on land and through the Great Lakes).</p>

            </div>
          

          
            <strong>Purpose</strong><br>
            <div class="small">
              
  <p>The primary purpose of this dataset is to update the official depiction of these maritime zones and maritime boundaries on NOAA&#39;s nautical charts.  The limits of these zones are subject to modification, as represented on future charts.  The limits shown on the most recent chart edition take precedence.</p>

            </div>
          

          

          <div class="cf"></div><!-- needed to clear browse image float -->

